  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) achieves 0.485 acc, while both image-only configs (0.208–0.227) are far weaker, establishing RNA as the dominant modality. However, we have only one RNA data point and PCA is a linear, unlearned projection. Question: Does a learned, biologically-informed RNA encoder (scvi_n200) produce a meaningfully better representation than PCA for the same singleprofile, rna_only setting? This is a clean one-variable comparison against config_001 (only the encoder changes). Why this is the best next step: The encoder choice is the most fundamental design axis—if a learned encoder substantially improves over PCA, it validates investing in scvi/scgpt across profile settings and multimodal fusion; if it does not, PCA suffices and we should pivot to multiprofile/aggregation or multimodal experiments. Resolving this uncertainty before testing aggregation or fusion avoids spending budget on downstream combinations built on a suboptimal representation."
}

